maya-cs: Quick triage - IgniteMobile onboarding: Android clients flooding init -> lots of 401 then 429. They report ~250 devices booting after an update.

ryan-platform: Are they using ephemerals or a single SDK key? Any logs you can paste?

maya-cs: They use ephemeral tokens from their /session-token endpoint. SDK v1.3.9. Log excerpt:

```
W/HttpClient: POST /v1/chat 401 unauthorized
W/HttpClient: fetching session token
W/HttpClient: POST /v1/chat 429 rate_limit window=60s
```

Pattern: token request, then a bunch of immediate generates that fail with 401 then rapid retries produce 429s. They didn't await token promise and call SDK.start from multiple entrypoints.

hao-eng: Sounds like a preflight stampede + connection pool behavior. If token is fetched asynchronously and many threads use the shared OkHttp pool without per-request Authorization, first few succeed but later ones race without header. Two checks: (1) confirm they await token before issuing user calls; (2) confirm Authorization header is added by an interceptor on every request.

ryan-platform: Also check client retry policy. If their retry loop immediately retries on 401 and replays identical requests, it magnifies the window. We usually recommend a single refresh+replay with backoff.

zoe-support: Triage notes we send customers in this case: throttle SDK.init to a single hot-path, enable client-side preflight coalescing, and add small randomized delay after token fetch before issuing generative calls. Example Android config we suggest:

```
// example
SdkOptions opts = new SdkOptions.Builder()
  .maxPreflightConcurrency(3)
  .enablePreflightCoalesce(true)
  .tokenAcquisitionJitterMs(1500)
  .retryOn401(false) // handle refresh+single-replay
  .build();

RedwoodMobile.init(context, opts);
```

Also recommend setting the connection pool size to 4 instead of default if devices spawn many short-lived threads.

maya-cs: They were calling init from splash screen, login activity, and a background sync at once. I'll ask them to gate init behind a single synchronized entrypoint. Any small code snippet?

hao-eng: Use an AtomicBoolean and a single-thread executor to serialize init/token refresh. Also ensure interceptor adds header per-request. Pseudocode:

```
private final AtomicBoolean initLock = new AtomicBoolean(false);
if (initLock.compareAndSet(false, true)) {
  singleThreadExecutor.execute(() -> {
    String token = blockingFetchToken();
    sdk.startWithToken(token);
    initLock.set(false);
  });
}
// OkHttp interceptor
public Response intercept(Chain chain) {
  Request req = chain.request().newBuilder()
    .header("Authorization", "Bearer " + tokenCache.get())
    .build();
  return chain.proceed(req);
}
```

ryan-platform: Short-term server-side workaround: have their /session-token endpoint cache a token for 2-5s when bursty requests detected, or queue token issuance behind a tiny semaphore. This smooths the initial fanout. Longer term: add mobile onboarding doc with code patterns.

zoe-support: I'll prepare a KB snippet and paste it to the customer with the changes. Also recommend upgrading to android-sdk 1.3.10 which fixes a race in the token refresh path.

alert-bot: [incident] edge.rate.ignite spike -> 429 rate_limit elevated by 380% over 5m. Alerting @ryan-platform

maya-cs: Customer applied single-init + small jitter (1.5s) and reduced preflight concurrency to 3. They reported 429s dropped to normal levels. Closing the loop; I'll add a short section to the onboarding checklist about mobile init patterns. Thanks team.
